       The iioopprriioo__ggeett() and iioopprriioo__sseett() system calls  get  and  set  the  I/O
       scheduling class and priority of one or more threads.

       The  _w_h_i_c_h and _w_h_o arguments identify the thread(s) on which the system
       calls operate.  The _w_h_i_c_h argument determines how _w_h_o  is  interpreted,
       and has one of the following values:

       IIOOPPRRIIOO__WWHHOO__PPRROOCCEESSSS
              _w_h_o is a process ID or thread ID identifying a single process or
              thread.  If _w_h_o is 0, then operate on the calling thread.

       IIOOPPRRIIOO__WWHHOO__PPGGRRPP
              _w_h_o  is  a  process  group  ID  identifying all the members of a
              process group.  If _w_h_o is 0, then operate on the  process  group
              of which the caller is a member.

       IIOOPPRRIIOO__WWHHOO__UUSSEERR
              _w_h_o  is  a  user ID identifying all of the processes that have a
              matching real UID.

       If _w_h_i_c_h is specified as IIOOPPRRIIOO__WWHHOO__PPGGRRPP or IIOOPPRRIIOO__WWHHOO__UUSSEERR when  call‐
       ing  iioopprriioo__ggeett(),  and more than one process matches _w_h_o, then the re‐
       turned priority will be the highest one found among all of the matching
       processes.  One priority is said to be higher than another  one  if  it
       belongs to a higher priority class (IIOOPPRRIIOO__CCLLAASSSS__RRTT is the highest pri‐
       ority  class;  IIOOPPRRIIOO__CCLLAASSSS__IIDDLLEE is the lowest) or if it belongs to the
       same priority class as the other process  but  has  a  higher  priority
       level (a lower priority number means a higher priority level).

