       Permission to change a process’s priority is granted or denied based on
       two criteria:

       PPrroocceessss oowwnneerrsshhiipp
              An  unprivileged  process  may  set  the I/O priority only for a
              process whose real UID matches the real or effective UID of  the
              calling  process.  A process which has the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__NNIICCEE capabil‐
              ity can change the priority of any process.

       WWhhaatt iiss tthhee ddeessiirreedd pprriioorriittyy
              Attempts to set very high priorities  (IIOOPPRRIIOO__CCLLAASSSS__RRTT)  require
              the  CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN capability.  Up to Linux 2.6.24 also required
              CCAAPP__SSYYSS__AADDMMIINN to set a very  low  priority  (IIOOPPRRIIOO__CCLLAASSSS__IIDDLLEE),
              but since Linux 2.6.25, this is no longer required.

       A  call  to  iioopprriioo__sseett() must follow both rules, or the call will fail
       with the error EEPPEERRMM.

